#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175756 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/id #uuid "98209a93-97d9-5068-bfb4-34c124bd2333" :question/math-academy-id "e-9042" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation\n$y'' - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = 7tx_{2} - e^{t}x_{1} - t\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a0b-777f-5d63-9f3a-3b3f4a6969d8" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Inhomogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f17360c7-9edb-50ec-b9d8-d8baad76a262" :question/math-academy-id "e-9139" :question/problem "Consider the third-order differential equation\n$y''' + t y' - 8 y = 1 - t^2$,\nwhere $y = y(t)$. If\n${y = x_{1}(t); y^{′} = x_{2}(t); y^{″} = x_{3}(t)$,\nthen the given differential equation can be rewritten as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nDetermine the matrices $A$ and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$." :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{3} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′} &= y^{″} \\\\\nx_{3}^{′} &= y^{‴}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' =-t y' + 8 y + 1 - t^2\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' =-t x_2 + 8 x_1 + 1 - t^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = 8x_{1} - tx_{2} + 1 - t^{2}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′} \\\\ x_{2}^{′} \\\\ x_{3}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -t & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82760ffd-6fde-5b2d-bc67-c7429a28c87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{t}x_{2} - x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173527} {:answer/id #uuid "8e30afae-4de5-5913-893b-c6f88af2ef58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{1} - \\sqrt{t}x_{2} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173528} {:answer/id #uuid "922bdfed-e539-5811-8344-f257de7c1f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{1} + \\sqrt{t}x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173529} {:answer/id #uuid "b7040b19-aee0-5538-993b-df1eaf5dd833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{t}x_{2} + x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173530} {:answer/id #uuid "00ef6877-32dd-5dbc-a3af-7a916ebf5d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x_{1} - \\sqrt{t}x_{3} - t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "922bdfed-e539-5811-8344-f257de7c1f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{1} + \\sqrt{t}x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173529} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28961f7-8cc1-5f29-a1d8-140658913d7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173526}] :question/id #uuid "cd48b46d-ec29-5080-9e57-ded7b691472a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107932" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y^{‴} - \\sqrt{t}y^{″} - y = t^{2}$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$ and $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a